 ],
 "literal": "Strong chariots to supplicants who gave, cool garlanded / Andiran is coming, saying, bright-bangled women / thunderbolt broad-handed tall lord's inside the palace / leather-covered drum also sounding / a roar of welcome arose indeed in the sky.",
 "translation": "As they say, 'Andiran of the cool garland, who gave strong chariots to those who begged, is coming,' inside the palace of the tall lord of the broad hand holding the thunderbolt, the leather-covered drums beat, and a great roar of welcome arose in the heavens.",
 "modern": "வலிமையான தேர்களை இரவலர்களுக்குக் கொடையாகக் கொடுத்த, குளிர்ந்த மாலையை அணிந்த ஆய் அண்டிரன் இறந்து விண்ணுலகிற்கு வருகிறான் என்று கூறி, வச்சிராயுதத்தை ஏந்திய பெரிய கைகளையுடைய இந்திரனின் அரண்மனைக்குள், தோல் போர்த்திய முரசுகள் ஒலிக்க, வானுலகில் பெரும் வரவேற்பு முழக்கம் எழுந்தது.",
 "explanation": "This poem belongs to the Potuviyal tiṇai and Kaiyaṟunilai theme, composed by Uṟaiyūr Ēṇiccēri Muṭamōciyār on the death of the patron chieftain Āy Aṇṭiraṉ. Instead of a standard earthly lament, the poet beautifully imagines the grand reception awaiting Āy in the heavens. The poet describes how the celestial maidens with bright bangles announce his arrival, saying, 'Andiran, who gave away strong chariots to supplicants, is coming.' Hearing this, inside the palace of Indra (the tall lord with the thunderbolt), the ceremonial drums are beaten, and a joyous roar of welcome echoes through the sky. This imagery highlights the immense generosity of Āy, suggesting that his fame and virtue are so great that even the gods celebrate his arrival in the afterlife.",
